    {
      "id": "revision_novelty_crosschannel_absorption_lag_j_1",
      "name": "Cross-Channel Revision Absorption Lag (jitter)",
      "class_name": "",
      "description": "Builds a signed, event-time EPS and revenue estimate-revision impulse, retaining only same-direction cross-channel updates and scaling them by each name's trailing revision variability. The factor favors unabsorbed positive impulses and shorts unabsorbed negative impulses after removing sector-relative price response.",
      "formula": "",
      "trading_idea": "Analyst revisions should drift most when EPS and revenue consensus move coherently, the update is unusually large relative to that firm's normal revision noise, and the stock has not yet incorporated the information relative to its sector. Cross-channel agreement makes a revision more likely to reflect a broad operating-narrative update rather than analyst-model noise, while muted initial price response identifies constrained or inattentive capital. The edge can persist because consensus updates arrive intermittently and portfolio managers often require confirmation before changing positions; it is falsified if coherent, novel revisions with weak relative price reaction do not outperform already-absorbed revisions over the next six sessions.",
      "trading_idea_ids": [],
