      "id": "workingcapital_tail_loading_derisking",
      "name": "Working-Capital Tail Loading",
      "class_name": "",
      "description": "Computes a causal lower-tail co-exceedance loading of each stock to the cross-sectional median market return, measures its recent innovation, and combines it with quarterly cash-conversion-cycle deterioration and leverage. The score is active only during a statistically meaningful market selloff; higher values forecast lower six-bar returns.",
      "formula": "",
      "trading_idea": "A deterioration in the cash-conversion cycle ties up cash in receivables and inventory, making a levered issuer especially vulnerable when aggregate downside risk rises. The signal identifies firms whose realized lower-tail co-movement with the market has recently increased and whose reported working-capital funding need has worsened; these names should continue to underperform during a de-risking episode. The edge should persist because quarterly working-capital disclosures are slow-moving while constrained intermediaries and risk-targeting investors reduce exposure to newly recognized high-tail-beta balance sheets rather than instantly arbitraging the pricing gap. This is falsifiable: conditional on market stress, subsequent returns should be most negative for the joint top bucket of cash-cycle expansion, leverage, and tail-coupling innovation, but not for any component alone.",
