      "id": "phase_plane_loop_terminal_dislocation_repair",
      "name": "Phase-Plane Liquidity Loop Repair",
      "class_name": "",
      "description": "Builds a closed-path signed area from normalized open-to-close returns and volume surprises, then multiplies loop intensity by the opposite of the recent standardized close-to-close displacement. High values identify downward terminal dislocations following strong price-volume phase loops and predict positive forward returns.",
      "formula": "",
      "trading_idea": "A large closed-loop area in the joint path of intrabar price changes and abnormal volume indicates that price movement and trading activity are temporally out of phase: liquidity is being replenished or cancelled after, rather than alongside, the initial price impulse. When such a loop ends with a substantial directional displacement, the terminal move is more likely to be an inventory-driven dislocation than permanent information, so the signal fades that displacement over the next six sessions. This is falsified if loop-conditioned reversals vanish after controlling for ordinary short-term reversal, realized volatility, and volume surprises; the effect can persist because the latent replenishment/cancellation process is not directly observable from daily bars and is costly to arbitrage precisely during stressed liquidity episodes.",
